  "worked_solution": "The center of the expansion is $x=1,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-3,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R\\ge 4.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-3,5).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-3,5).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227049/assets/1f6d98b7983946bf60df9bb29bc527af17a63a38834457404c5284b19165e7d5.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $5\\notin [-3,5),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-4\\notin [-3,5),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $-2\\in [-3,5),$ the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
